<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-glutamate biosynthesis V" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-4341" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Glutamate synthase (glutamine-oxoglutarate aminotransferase, GOGAT) is an important enzyme involved in ammonia (nitrogen) assimilation. Many plants, including Arabidopsis, have both NADH-dependent and ferredoxin-dependent GOGAT enzymes. The ferredoxin-dependent GOGAT is the predominant form found in Arabidopsis. Ferredoxin-dependent enzymes are also found in |FRAME: TAX-1117|, such as |FRAME: TAX-1148| |CITS: [7727752]|. There are two genes in Arabidopsis encoding Fd-GOGAT. |FRAME: AT5G04140| is primarily expressed in the leaves and its expression is induced by light and sucrose, whereas GLU2 is preferentially expressed in roots. |FRAME: AT5G04140| plays a major role in assimilating the large amount of ammonia released from photorespiration and is also involved in primary nitrogen assimilation in leaves. GLU2 may be involved in primary nitrogen assimilation in roots |CITS:[SOMERVILLE80][9057836][9596633]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
